  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Split porecrust (Xylodon paradoxus) is a small, inconspicuous wood-decay fungus belonging to the Schizoporaceae family, characterized by its unique pore structure that appears to split or tear. This corticioid fungus plays an important role in decomposing dead wood in forest ecosystems.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Split Pores",
      "description": "The hymenium features distinctive angular pores that often appear cracked, split, or irregularly lacerated, giving the fungus its common name",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Thin Crust",
      "description": "Forms a thin, resupinate (flat against substrate) crust that is typically whitish to pale cream-colored",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Substrate Preference",
      "description": "Found exclusively on hardwood, particularly on decaying branches and logs in moist conditions",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Split porecrust grows on dead hardwood substrates in moist forest environments throughout New England, preferring shaded areas with high humidity.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "The 'paradoxus' in its scientific name refers to the puzzling nature of its pore structure, which combines features intermediate between polypores and corticioid fungi, making it a taxonomically interesting species.",
